    <string name="heir_tile_setup">Set up an heir</string>
    <string name="heir_tile_configured">Heir set up</string>
    <string name="heir_tile_subtitle_setup">Someone close to you can reach the vault after a long period of inactivity. No cloud, no third party.</string>
    <string name="heir_tile_subtitle_configured">Tap to update the snapshot or turn it off</string>
    <!--
      The last bullet is not 2.7.1's. It claimed the snapshot was detectable, which was true of its
      single file; here the three exist and look alike, so what a phone shows is that AN heir was set
      up, never for which vault.
    -->
    <string name="heir_setup_body">A password of its own, different from yours, lets someone close to you (partner, child, executor) reach the contents of the vault **after a long period of inactivity** on your part (90 days by default, plus 7 days of grace).\n\nKeep in mind:\n• The heir password must differ from your master password\n• Give it to your heir safely (in person, in a will, in a bank vault) — it is never stored and cannot be recovered\n• The heir reads a snapshot of the vault. Remember to update it from time to time.\n• If you unlock the vault before the grace ends, the countdown starts again.\n• All three snapshots then exist and look alike: nothing says which of your vaults the real one belongs to. That an heir is set up on this phone does show to an examination — for deniability, see the decoy vault.\n\nThis works entirely on the phone, with no cloud and no trusted third party.</string>
